module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.CokernelFinitePresentation
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.EffectiveCartierDivisor
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeProperties

/-!
# The four assertions of Stacks 0846 about the zero scheme of a section

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
`𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  Stacks 0846 makes four assertions about
the zero scheme `Z(s)` of Stacks 0845:

1. among the closed immersions `i : Z ⟶ X` with `i^*s = 0`, ordered by inclusion, `Z(s)` is the
   greatest;
2. a morphism `f : Y ⟶ X` of algebraic spaces satisfies `f^*s = 0` exactly when it factors
   through `Z(s)`;
3. `Z(s)` is a locally principal closed subspace of `X`;
4. `Z(s)` is an effective Cartier divisor exactly when `s` is a regular section of `L`.

This file states the assertions that carry hypotheses the source does not and places the
Stacks 0846 tag on them; each clause label names the hypotheses, and the implementation notes
say what they are and why they are not removable with what the library has.  The one half of
part (4) that needs no such hypothesis is tagged where it is proved, in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isGreatest_zeroScheme_of_exists_definingIdealAt_eq` is part (1).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_comp_eq_zeroScheme_iff_of_exists_definingIdealAt_eq` is
  part (2).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocallyPrincipal_zeroScheme_of_epi_inverseTensorPower` is part (3).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq_span_generator` is the first half of
  part (4): the zero scheme is cut out on each affine étale chart by a single regular function.
  Its second half, that `s` is a regular section exactly when each of those functions is a
  nonzerodivisor, needs no hypothesis on the zero scheme and is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isRegularSection_iff_forall_injective_mul_generator` in
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean`, where it carries the
  Stacks 0846 tag for that half.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isLocallyGeneratedByOnSite_of_epi`: local generation by `r` sections
  passes to the target of an epimorphism.  This is the one general fact part (3) needs.

## Implementation notes

**The hypothesis of parts (1), (2) and (4).** `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme` is defined,
in `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroScheme.lean`, as the intersection of the closed
subspaces whose defining ideal is contained in the ideal
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt` generated by the local evaluations of `s`.
Nothing about an intersection forces its defining ideal to *contain* that evaluation ideal, and
when the family is `{X}` the intersection is `X` itself, whose defining ideal is zero.  So these
three parts carry the hypothesis that *some* closed subspace of `X` has the evaluation ideal of
`s` as its defining ideal on the affine étale charts.  That is exactly what the surjectivity
half of Stacks 03MB supplies — a quasi-coherent ideal of `𝒪_X` is the defining ideal of a
closed subspace — and that half is proved, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealSheaf_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`
(`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ClosedSubspaceFromQuasicoherentIdeal.lean:329`), with
Stacks 03MB registered on
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedImmersion_quasicoherentIdeal_correspondence`
(`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ClosedImmersionIdealCorrespondence.lean:220`).  For an
invertible `L` the hypothesis is discharged in the chartwise form it is stated in here, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible`
(`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeIdealSheaf.lean:387`).  It is carried as an
existential, so it is that input verbatim and not a choice of subspace;
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq` then identifies `Z(s)` with that subspace.

**The chartwise reading of vanishing in parts (1) and (2).** "The section pulls back to zero
along `i : Z ⟶ X`" is read as the containment of the evaluation ideal of `s` in the defining
ideal of `Z` on every affine étale chart, and for part (2) as the vanishing of the evaluation
ideal after pullback of regular functions along every point of `Y` over an affine chart.  The
equivalence of either reading with the vanishing of the pulled-back section in the sense of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullback` is not available; see the implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeFactorization.lean`.  The scheme-level
statement, Stacks 02OR, is proved with the genuine module-theoretic vanishing on both sides as
`AlgebraicGeometry.pullbackSection_eq_zero_iff_factorsThrough_zeroScheme`, so it is the
comparison carrying chart ideals onto the small étale site of `X` that is missing, not the
scheme-level mathematics.

**Part (3).** `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsLocallyPrincipal` of Stacks 083B is a condition on the
defining ideal *sheaf* `𝓘_{Z(s)}` on the small étale site: it is locally generated by one
section.  In the source `𝓘_{Z(s)}` is the image of the evaluation map `L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝒪_X` of
Stacks 0845, and local principality is then local triviality of the invertible module
`L^{⊗ -1}` transported along that surjection.  Neither input is available: the image
factorization of the evaluation map is again the surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB, and local
triviality of an invertible module on a site is the descent clause of Stacks 0B8M, which this
library has only for a ringed space whose stalks are local rings (see the section "What is not
proved" of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/EffectiveCartierEtaleCharts.lean`).  Both are
therefore hypotheses: an epimorphism `p : L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝓘_{Z(s)}`, and local generation of
`L^{⊗ -1}` by one section.  The source of `p` is pinned to the inverse tensor power
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Invertible.tensorPower` of Stacks 01CU and not left arbitrary, because
for an arbitrary source the hypothesis would be satisfied by `𝓘_{Z(s)}` itself and the identity
and so would say no more than the conclusion.  Both hypotheses are used by the proof, through
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isLocallyGeneratedByOnSite_of_epi`, whose content is that restriction to
a slice site preserves epimorphisms,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalCokernel.overFunctor_preservesEpimorphisms`, so that Mathlib's
`SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections.ofEpi` applies on each member of the covering sieve with the
index type unchanged.

**Part (4) is read chartwise, and this is a weakening of the conclusion, not only of the
hypotheses.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor` of Stacks 083B asks that
`𝓘_{Z(s)}` be an invertible `𝒪_X`-module.  Once `𝓘_{Z(s)}` is identified with the evaluation
ideal, invertibility of that sheaf is still not in reach: it would follow from the evaluation
map `L^{⊗ -1} ⟶ 𝓘_{Z(s)}` being an isomorphism, i.e. from that map being both epi (Stacks
03MB again) and mono (regularity of `s`), and an epi-and-mono hypothesis on a map onto
`𝓘_{Z(s)}` from an invertible module is equivalent to invertibility of `𝓘_{Z(s)}`, so it cannot
be assumed.  What is stated instead is the chartwise content of part (4): on an affine étale
chart where `L` is free of rank one the zero scheme is cut out by the single regular function
`l₀(s|_U)`, and `s` is a regular section in the sense of Stacks 0843 exactly when multiplication
by each of those functions is injective.

Freeness of `L` on a chart is carried as a generator `t U` of `L(U)` together with a linear
functional `l₀ U` satisfying `l₀ U x • t U = x`; no normalisation at `t U` is needed.  For an
invertible `L` this holds locally, which is what the source uses it for.

